/**
@mainpage Smart Meter Firmware

@tableofcontents

@section intro Overview

Firmware for a battery-powered **NB-IoT water meter**. The device counts pulses
from a water-meter pulse emitter, keeps a running volume total, and periodically
reports it to a **head-end system (HES)** over a cellular NB-IoT link. It is
designed for **ultra-low power** so that a single primary battery lasts the
target service life (> 8 years): the MCU spends almost all of its time in STOP
mode and only wakes to count a pulse or to run a short reporting session.

Key characteristics:
- Event-driven pulse counting with hardware-independent debouncing.
- Non-blocking finite-state machines for registration and periodic sessions.
- Persistent credentials and pulse count across resets and power loss.
- Robustness: exponential backoff, watchdog, and MCU reset on unrecoverable
  hardware faults.

@section hw Hardware

| Block            | Part / detail |
|------------------|---------------|
| MCU              | STM32L031K6 (Cortex-M0+, 32 KB Flash, 8 KB RAM, 1 KB Data EEPROM) |
| Cellular modem   | Quectel BG95-M3 (NB-IoT) |
| Real-time clock  | STM32 **internal** RTC, clocked from an external LSE 32.768 kHz crystal (LSE CSS enabled) |
| Persistence      | STM32 **internal** Data EEPROM (no external EEPROM) |
| Pulse input      | Pulse emitter on **PA8** (EXTI) |
| Modem control    | `PB4` STATUS (in), `PB5` PWRKEY (out), level-shifter enable (GPIO) |
| Level shifter    | 3.3 V ↔ 1.8 V between MCU and BG95 |
| Power            | Primary battery + supercapacitor for the BG95 transmit peaks |

@note The RTC and the persistent memory are **internal** to the STM32L031;
there is no external RTC/EEPROM and no I2C bus involved.

@section arch Architecture

The code is organised in layers. See the @ref app "Application modules" and
@ref drivers "Device drivers" pages for the full API.

@verbatim
  +-------------------------------------------------------------+
  |  Application  |  ATCom (registration / session / UDP)       |
  |  modules      |  Payload/RLP · PulseCounter · Storage · LPM |
  +-------------------------------------------------------------+
  |  Drivers      |  ATCore (AT framework)  ·  BG95 (modem)     |
  +-------------------------------------------------------------+
  |  Platform     |  STM32L0 HAL + CubeMX-generated peripherals |
  +-------------------------------------------------------------+
@endverbatim

- **Application modules** (@ref app) implement the device behaviour.
- **Device drivers** (@ref drivers) abstract the modem and the AT transport.
- The **platform layer** (HAL, clock/peripheral init, ISRs, startup) is
  generated by STM32CubeMX; see @ref generated below.

@section lifecycle Operating cycle

After initialization the device either registers (first boot) or goes straight
to sleep. From then on it alternates between STOP-mode sleep and short HES
sessions triggered by the RTC alarm, waking briefly on each water pulse to
update the counter.

@image html firmware-lifecycle.png "Top-level firmware lifecycle" width=55%

@section fsms Communication state machines

Both communication phases are non-blocking FSMs (see @ref atcom). They share the
@ref atcom_udp "UDP context" setup and the same error policy: on error they
apply an exponential backoff and, after N consecutive failures, reset the MCU.

@subsection fsm_reg Registration

Run once, on first boot, until the device is enrolled and its credentials are
stored in EEPROM.

@image html firmware-registration-fsm.png "Registration FSM" width=48%

@subsection fsm_ses Session

Run on every wake-up to exchange data with the HES: announce the current IP
(NAT pinhole for CGNAT), then service HES requests until the HES acknowledges.

@image html firmware-session-fsm.png "Periodic session FSM" width=60%

@section protocol HES protocol (summary)

Messages are exchanged over UDP as an **envelope** (46-byte header:
version, message type, device id, MAC, sequence number, timestamp) followed by
an **RLP-encoded** body of OBIS code/value pairs. See @ref payload.

Message types used by the firmware include handshake, registration
(request/response/ACK), IP announce, and read/write requests. OBIS codes
include water volume (`1.0.1`), clock (`0.9.4`) and battery level (`C.6.1`).

@section generated Generated (CubeMX/HAL) code

The low-level platform code — the STM32L0 HAL driver, `SystemClock_Config`,
peripheral initialization (`MX_*_Init` for USART/LPUART, DMA, RTC, IWDG, GPIO)
and the interrupt vector table — is **generated by STM32CubeMX** and is not
documented in detail here. Application-specific logic added inside those files
lives in the CubeMX `USER CODE BEGIN/END` guards and, where relevant, is
documented (e.g. the pulse EXTI handler and the RTC helpers). This documentation
focuses on the hand-written application modules and drivers.

@section build Building

From `SM_Project/` (ARM GCC toolchain on PATH):

@code{.sh}
cmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=cmake/gcc-arm-none-eabi.cmake -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug
cmake --build build
@endcode

To regenerate this documentation, run `doxygen Doxyfile` from the `Doc/`
directory (requires Doxygen and Graphviz `dot`).
*/
